// THE REP CLIFF IS CLOSED. This file was `@emit-error annotate the return type` — a loud
// refusal with a one-word remedy — and before that refusal existed it printed `0` where
// `circle` was expected, at rc 0 with a valid module.
//
// `function describe(s: S) { return s.kind }` returns the tag with no annotation. When the
// arms' tag fields are BARE literals the read is a plain string and the function always ran
// unannotated. When they are NAMED litunion aliases the same read yields the interned i32
// ATOM — and the join TYPE is `"circle" | "rect"` in BOTH cases, so nothing downstream could
// tell them apart from the type.
//
// WHAT THE TYPE COULD NOT CARRY, A FLAG DOES. The two passes that had to agree are the
// SIGNATURE (classified from the returned expression by `criClassify`, since the A20 inferred
// -return row for such a function is EMPTY — measured, `inferRetRowOf` and `inferRetRowByNode`
// both answer -1) and the CALL SITE (which must widen an atom before `print`). `fRetKind`
// cannot be the meeting point: both reps ARE i32, so the kind is `"i32"` either way. So a
// parallel `fRetLitAtom` records the one bit the kind cannot, and `exprIsLitAtom`'s
// un-annotated-callee arm reads it.
//
// IT IS RECORDED IN A SEPARATE PASS, AFTER the kinds settle, and that placement is measured
// rather than chosen. Asking the same question inside `criClassify` moved
// `unions/atom-string-join.vl` from green to `print of a literal-union atom whose type
// carries no member texts`: that classifier runs to a FIXPOINT and its arms feed each other,
// so a question asked inside it is not free of the loop it sits in.
//
// AND IT IS GATED ON THE SETTLED KIND BEING `i32`. A function whose returns JOIN an atom with
// a string (`if c { return k }  return "cc"`) settles at `"str"` and widens the atom at its
// OWN return, so its result is a string ref — and `fnRetExprOf` picks only one return, which
// may be the atom arm. Without the gate that function was flagged and its callers widened a
// value already widened, which is the same fixture going red a second way. The kind is the
// settled answer for ALL returns; the expression test distinguishes an atom from a plain i32.
// @run
// @log circle
// @log rect
// @log <circle>
// @log eq
// @log 42
type KC = "circle"
type KR = "rect"
type C = { kind: KC, r: f64 }
type R = { kind: KR, w: f64 }
type S = C | R

// the tag returned with NO annotation, over ATOM-repped arms
function describe(s: S) { return s.kind }
print(describe({ kind: "circle", r: 1.0 }))
print(describe({ kind: "rect", w: 2.0 }))

// and consumed as the string it reads as
print("<" + describe({ kind: "circle", r: 1.0 }) + ">")
if describe({ kind: "rect", w: 1.0 }) == "rect" { print("eq") } else { print("ne") }

// a plain i32 return must NOT be widened — the flag is what separates them, since both reps
// leave the kind at `i32`
function inc(n: i32) { return n + 1 }
print(inc(41))
